## 3. Main Theorem: Monotonicity of the Viability Kernel

### Theorem 1 (Power–Governance Monotonicity)

Assume A1 and A2. Fix \(\rho,\xi,\tau\) and \(x_0\in\mathcal S\). Then:

**(i) Power monotonicity.**

\[
\mathbf P_2\succeq\mathbf P_1
\;\Longrightarrow\;
\mathcal K(\mathbf P_2,\mathbf G,\rho,\xi,\tau)
\subseteq
\mathcal K(\mathbf P_1,\mathbf G,\rho,\xi,\tau).
\]

**(ii) Governance monotonicity.**

\[
\mathbf G_2\succeq\mathbf G_1
\;\Longrightarrow\;
\mathcal K(\mathbf P,\mathbf G_2,\rho,\xi,\tau)
\supseteq
\mathcal K(\mathbf P,\mathbf G_1,\rho,\xi,\tau).
\]

### Proof

**(i)** Let \(x_0\in\mathcal K(\mathbf P_2,\mathbf G,\rho,\xi,\tau)\). By definition, there exists a nonanticipative policy \(\pi\) with \(u(t)\in\mathcal U(\mathbf G)\) such that for every \(d(\cdot)\in\mathcal D(\mathbf P_2,\rho,\xi,\tau)\), the trajectory remains in \(\mathcal S\). By A1, \(\mathcal D(\mathbf P_1,\rho,\xi,\tau)\subseteq\mathcal D(\mathbf P_2,\rho,\xi,\tau)\). Therefore the same policy \(\pi\) also keeps the trajectory in \(\mathcal S\) against every \(d(\cdot)\in\mathcal D(\mathbf P_1,\rho,\xi,\tau)\). Hence \(x_0\in\mathcal K(\mathbf P_1,\mathbf G,\rho,\xi,\tau)\).

**(ii)** Let \(x_0\in\mathcal K(\mathbf P,\mathbf G_1,\rho,\xi,\tau)\). There exists \(\pi\) with \(u(t)\in\mathcal U(\mathbf G_1)\) keeping the trajectory in \(\mathcal S\) against all \(d(\cdot)\in\mathcal D(\mathbf P,\rho,\xi,\tau)\). By A2, \(\mathcal U(\mathbf G_1)\subseteq\mathcal U(\mathbf G_2)\), so the same policy is admissible for \(\mathbf G_2\). Hence \(x_0\in\mathcal K(\mathbf P,\mathbf G_2,\rho,\xi,\tau)\). \(\square\)

**Remark 3.1.** The theorem is not a definition. It is a monotonicity statement that requires A1 and A2 and fails if either fails. The proof is short because the content is in the assumptions and in the choice of the robust viability kernel as the primitive object.

### Corollary 1 (Minimum governance threshold)

For fixed \(\mathbf P,\rho,\xi,\tau\) and \(x_0\in\mathcal S\), define the minimum governance threshold

\[
\mathbf G_{\min}(\mathbf P,\rho,\xi,\tau;x_0)
=
\inf_{\succeq}
\left\{
\mathbf G\in\mathcal M:
x_0\in\mathcal K(\mathbf P,\mathbf G,\rho,\xi,\tau)
\right\},
\]

where the infimum is taken over the partial order \(\succeq\) on the feasible governance manifold \(\mathcal M\). When a unique minimum does not exist, \(\mathbf G_{\min}\) is understood as the set of Pareto-minimal viable governance vectors, and the synchronization condition requires that \(\mathbf G(t)\) dominate some element of this set.

Then, under A1 and A2,

\[
\boxed{
\mathbf P_2\succeq\mathbf P_1
\;\Longrightarrow\;
\mathbf G_{\min}(\mathbf P_2,\rho,\xi,\tau;x_0)
\succeq
\mathbf G_{\min}(\mathbf P_1,\rho,\xi,\tau;x_0)
}
\]

whenever both thresholds exist.

### Proof

By Theorem 1(i), \(\mathcal K(\mathbf P_2,\mathbf G)\subseteq\mathcal K(\mathbf P_1,\mathbf G)\) for every \(\mathbf G\). Therefore the set of governance vectors that render \(x_0\) viable under \(\mathbf P_2\) is a subset of the corresponding set under \(\mathbf P_1\). The infimum over a smaller upper set is \(\succeq\)-greater. \(\square\)

**Remark 3.2.** The corollary is the precise form of the synchronization condition. It replaces the earlier formulation \(\mathbf G(t)\succeq\mathbf G_{\min}(\mathbf P(t),\rho(t),\xi(t),\tau)\) with a statement that is derived, not postulated.

---

## 4. Synchronization Condition

Combining Theorem 1 and Corollary 1, the synchronization condition is:

\[
\boxed{
\mathbf G(t)\succeq\mathbf G_{\min}\bigl(\mathbf P(t),\rho(t),\xi(t),\tau;x(t)\bigr).
}
\]

This is the central necessary condition for the modeled safety guarantee: the current governance capacity must dominate the minimum governance threshold required for \(x(t)\) to lie in the robust viability kernel under the current power vector and risk environment.

