## 6. Dynamic Synchronization Law

Suppose power grows: \(\dot{\mathbf P}(t)\succeq 0\). By Corollary 1, \(\mathbf G_{\min}\) is non-decreasing in \(\mathbf P\). If \(\mathbf G_{\min}\) is differentiable in its arguments, then

\[
\dot{\mathbf G}_{\min}
=
\frac{\partial\mathbf G_{\min}}{\partial\mathbf P}\dot{\mathbf P}
+
\frac{\partial\mathbf G_{\min}}{\partial\rho}\dot\rho
+
\frac{\partial\mathbf G_{\min}}{\partial\xi}\dot\xi
+
\frac{\partial\mathbf G_{\min}}{\partial\tau}\dot\tau.
\]

The dynamic synchronization condition is

\[
\boxed{
\dot{\mathbf G}(t)
\succeq
\frac{\partial\mathbf G_{\min}}{\partial\mathbf P}\dot{\mathbf P}
+
\frac{\partial\mathbf G_{\min}}{\partial\rho}\dot\rho
+
\frac{\partial\mathbf G_{\min}}{\partial\xi}\dot\xi
+
\frac{\partial\mathbf G_{\min}}{\partial\tau}\dot\tau.
}
\]

In margin form,

\[
\boxed{
\dot m(t)+\alpha(m(t))\ge 0.
}
\]

**Open problem 6.1 (Superlinear growth).** Prove or disprove the superlinear growth conjecture

\[
\frac{\partial^2 G_{\min}^{(j)}}{\partial \mathrm{Aut}^2}\ge 0,
\qquad
\frac{\partial^2 G_{\min}^{(j)}}{\partial I^2}\ge 0
\]

for each component \(G_{\min}^{(j)}\) of the minimum governance threshold, i.e. that required governance grows superlinearly in autonomy and irreversibility. If true, this would explain why incremental increases in autonomy or irreversibility can produce disproportionate governance requirements.

**Open problem 6.2 (Topological bifurcations of the kernel).** Under what conditions on \(\mathbf P\) and \(\mathbf G\) does the viability kernel \(\mathcal K\) undergo a sudden topological bifurcation—shattering into disconnected components or emptying entirely—as \(\mathbf P\) crosses a critical threshold? This question connects the framework to catastrophe theory and would explain sudden losses of viability that are not captured by continuous changes in the synchronization margin. A bifurcation of this kind would correspond to a discontinuous jump in the required governance threshold, which would render gradualist governance strategies insufficient.

