## 7. AI as Both Power and Governance Amplifier

Artificial intelligence can increase both \(\mathbf P\) and \(\mathbf G\). A candidate coupled-growth model is

\[
\dot P = r_P P\bigl(1+\eta_G G_{\text{power}}\bigr),
\]

\[
\dot G = r_G G + r_{\text{AI}} P G_{\text{gov}} - \delta G,
\]

where:

- \(G_{\text{power}}\) is AI used to amplify power,
- \(G_{\text{gov}}\) is AI used to amplify governance,
- \(\delta\) is governance decay or erosion.

These equations are a modeling hypothesis whose coefficients must be empirically estimated. The synchronization condition becomes

\[
r_G G + r_{\text{AI}} P G_{\text{gov}} - \delta G
\;\succeq\;
\frac{\partial\mathbf G_{\min}}{\partial\mathbf P}\dot{\mathbf P}
+
\frac{\partial\mathbf G_{\min}}{\partial\rho}\dot\rho
+
\frac{\partial\mathbf G_{\min}}{\partial\xi}\dot\xi.
\]

The central AI question is therefore:

\[
\boxed{
\text{Does AI increase effective governance capacity at least sufficiently}
}
\]

\[
\boxed{
\text{to compensate for the additional power it creates?}
}
\]

This is a measurable research direction, not a theorem. The stability of the coupled-growth model depends on the coefficients. If \(\dot P/P>\dot G/G\) for all \(G\) in a neighborhood of the current state, then power grows faster than governance, and the synchronization margin eventually becomes negative. This is a testable prediction.

