## 8. Multiplanetary and Latency-Constrained Governance

Let communication delay be \(\tau>0\). Correction capacity becomes delay-dependent:

\[
K=K(\tau).
\]

A plausible decay model is

\[
K(\tau)=K_0\left(1+\frac{\tau}{\tau_0}\right)^{-\beta},
\qquad \beta>0.
\]

Effective governance at a local settlement is

\[
\mathbf G_{\text{eff}}(\tau)
=
\mathbf G_{\text{local}}
+
e^{-\lambda\tau}\mathbf G_{\text{Earth}}.
\]

For a multiplanetary civilization, the synchronization condition becomes local:

\[
\boxed{
\mathbf G_{\text{local}}
\succeq
\mathbf G_{\min}\bigl(
\mathbf P_{\text{local}},
\rho_{\text{local}},
\xi_{\text{local}},
\tau;
x_{\text{local}}
\bigr).
}
\]

During communication blackouts—such as solar conjunction—Earth-based correction is unavailable. Therefore local viability kernels must be nonempty without real-time Earth intervention. As noted in §2.8, when \(\tau>0\) the state space is \(C([-\tau,0],\mathbb R^n)\), and the viability kernel \(\mathcal K_\tau\) is defined over this function space. Existence and approximation results for delay viability kernels are available in the viability-theory literature. This is an architectural constraint, not a philosophical one.

