## Appendix A: Detailed Proofs of Main Results

### A.1 Proof of Theorem 1 (Power–Governance Monotonicity)

**Theorem 1.** Assume A1 and A2. Fix \(\rho,\xi,\tau\) and \(x_0\in\mathcal S\). Then:

**(i) Power monotonicity.**
\[
\mathbf P_2\succeq\mathbf P_1
\;\Longrightarrow\;
\mathcal K(\mathbf P_2,\mathbf G,\rho,\xi,\tau)
\subseteq
\mathcal K(\mathbf P_1,\mathbf G,\rho,\xi,\tau).
\]

**(ii) Governance monotonicity.**
\[
\mathbf G_2\succeq\mathbf G_1
\;\Longrightarrow\;
\mathcal K(\mathbf P,\mathbf G_2,\rho,\xi,\tau)
\supseteq
\mathcal K(\mathbf P,\mathbf G_1,\rho,\xi,\tau).
\]

**Proof.**

**(i)** Let \(x_0\in\mathcal K(\mathbf P_2,\mathbf G,\rho,\xi,\tau)\). By definition, there exists a nonanticipative policy \(\pi\) with \(u(t)=\pi(x_{[0,t]},y_{[0,t]})\in\mathcal U(\mathbf G)\) such that for every \(d(\cdot)\in\mathcal D(\mathbf P_2,\rho,\xi,\tau)\), the trajectory \(x(t)\) satisfies \(x(t)\in\mathcal S\) for all \(t\ge 0\).

By Assumption A1, \(\mathbf P_2\succeq\mathbf P_1\) implies
\[
\mathcal D(\mathbf P_1,\rho,\xi,\tau)\subseteq\mathcal D(\mathbf P_2,\rho,\xi,\tau).
\]
Therefore, the same policy \(\pi\) also keeps the trajectory in \(\mathcal S\) against every disturbance \(d(\cdot)\in\mathcal D(\mathbf P_1,\rho,\xi,\tau)\). Hence \(x_0\in\mathcal K(\mathbf P_1,\mathbf G,\rho,\xi,\tau)\). This proves (i).

**(ii)** Let \(x_0\in\mathcal K(\mathbf P,\mathbf G_1,\rho,\xi,\tau)\). There exists a nonanticipative policy \(\pi\) with \(u(t)\in\mathcal U(\mathbf G_1)\) such that for every \(d(\cdot)\in\mathcal D(\mathbf P,\rho,\xi,\tau)\), the trajectory remains in \(\mathcal S\).

By Assumption A2, \(\mathbf G_2\succeq\mathbf G_1\) implies
\[
\mathcal U(\mathbf G_1)\subseteq\mathcal U(\mathbf G_2).
\]
Thus the same policy \(\pi\) is admissible for \(\mathbf G_2\), i.e., \(u(t)\in\mathcal U(\mathbf G_2)\). Therefore \(x_0\in\mathcal K(\mathbf P,\mathbf G_2,\rho,\xi,\tau)\). This proves (ii). \(\square\)

### A.2 Proof of Corollary 1 (Minimum Governance Threshold)

**Corollary 1.** For fixed \(\mathbf P,\rho,\xi,\tau\) and \(x_0\in\mathcal S\), define
\[
\mathbf G_{\min}(\mathbf P,\rho,\xi,\tau;x_0)
=
\inf_{\succeq}
\left\{
\mathbf G\in\mathcal M:
x_0\in\mathcal K(\mathbf P,\mathbf G,\rho,\xi,\tau)
\right\},
\]
where the infimum is taken over the partial order \(\succeq\) on the feasible governance manifold \(\mathcal M\). When a unique minimum does not exist, \(\mathbf G_{\min}\) is understood as the set of Pareto-minimal viable governance vectors.

Then, under A1 and A2,
\[
\mathbf P_2\succeq\mathbf P_1
\;\Longrightarrow\;
\mathbf G_{\min}(\mathbf P_2,\rho,\xi,\tau;x_0)
\succeq
\mathbf G_{\min}(\mathbf P_1,\rho,\xi,\tau;x_0)
\]
whenever both thresholds exist.

**Proof.** By Theorem 1(i), for every \(\mathbf G\in\mathcal M\),
\[
\mathcal K(\mathbf P_2,\mathbf G,\rho,\xi,\tau)
\subseteq
\mathcal K(\mathbf P_1,\mathbf G,\rho,\xi,\tau).
\]
Therefore, the set of governance vectors that render \(x_0\) viable under \(\mathbf P_2\),
\[
\Phi(\mathbf P_2) = \{\mathbf G\in\mathcal M: x_0\in\mathcal K(\mathbf P_2,\mathbf G,\rho,\xi,\tau)\},
\]
is a subset of the corresponding set under \(\mathbf P_1\),
\[
\Phi(\mathbf P_1) = \{\mathbf G\in\mathcal M: x_0\in\mathcal K(\mathbf P_1,\mathbf G,\rho,\xi,\tau)\}.
\]
That is, \(\Phi(\mathbf P_2)\subseteq\Phi(\mathbf P_1)\).

Both \(\Phi(\mathbf P_1)\) and \(\Phi(\mathbf P_2)\) are upper sets with respect to the partial order \(\succeq\) (by Theorem 1(ii)). The infimum over an upper set with respect to a partial order is its Pareto-minimal element (or set of elements). Since \(\Phi(\mathbf P_2)\subseteq\Phi(\mathbf P_1)\), the infimum over the smaller set is \(\succeq\)-greater. Hence
\[
\mathbf G_{\min}(\mathbf P_2)
\succeq
\mathbf G_{\min}(\mathbf P_1).
\]
\(\square\)

### A.3 Lemma: Monotonicity of the Viable Governance Set

**Lemma A.1.** For fixed \(\mathbf P,\rho,\xi,\tau\), the viable governance set
\[
\Phi(\mathbf P) = \{\mathbf G\in\mathcal M: x_0\in\mathcal K(\mathbf P,\mathbf G,\rho,\xi,\tau)\}
\]
is an upper set with respect to the partial order \(\succeq\) on \(\mathcal M\). That is, if \(\mathbf G_1\in\Phi(\mathbf P)\) and \(\mathbf G_2\succeq\mathbf G_1\) with \(\mathbf G_2\in\mathcal M\), then \(\mathbf G_2\in\Phi(\mathbf P)\).

**Proof.** If \(\mathbf G_1\in\Phi(\mathbf P)\), then \(x_0\in\mathcal K(\mathbf P,\mathbf G_1,\rho,\xi,\tau)\). By Theorem 1(ii), \(\mathbf G_2\succeq\mathbf G_1\) implies
\[
\mathcal K(\mathbf P,\mathbf G_1,\rho,\xi,\tau)
\subseteq
\mathcal K(\mathbf P,\mathbf G_2,\rho,\xi,\tau).
\]
Therefore \(x_0\in\mathcal K(\mathbf P,\mathbf G_2,\rho,\xi,\tau)\), so \(\mathbf G_2\in\Phi(\mathbf P)\). \(\square\)

### A.4 Proof of Theorem 2 (Viability Synchronization)

**Theorem 2.** Suppose the hypotheses of Theorem 1 hold, \(b\) is locally Lipschitz on \(\mathcal Z\), and there exists a nonanticipative policy \(\pi\) with \(u(t)\in\mathcal U(\mathbf G(t))\) such that
\[
\max_{v\in\partial_C b(z)}
\left(
v\cdot f(z,u,d)
\right)
+
\alpha(b(z))
\ge 0
\qquad
\forall d\in\mathcal D(\mathbf P,\rho,\xi,\tau).
\]
Then \(\mathcal S\) is forward invariant on \([t_0,t_1]\).

**Proof.** The condition
\[
\max_{v\in\partial_C b(z)}
\left(
v\cdot f(z,u,d)
\right)
+
\alpha(b(z))
\ge 0
\]
for all \(d\in\mathcal D(\mathbf P,\rho,\xi,\tau)\) is a non-smooth control barrier function condition. By Clarke's theorem on generalized gradients and Filippov solutions, this condition implies that the set \(\{z: b(z)\ge 0\}\) is forward invariant under the differential inclusion
\[
\dot z \in \{f(z,u,d): d\in\mathcal D(\mathbf P,\rho,\xi,\tau)\}.
\]
Since \(b(z)=m(t)=\operatorname{sdist}(\mathbf G,\Phi)\) and \(m\ge 0\) if and only if \(\mathbf G\in\Phi\), which is equivalent to \(x\in\mathcal K\), the forward invariance of \(\{b\ge 0\}\) implies that \(x(t)\in\mathcal S\) for all \(t\in[t_0,t_1]\). \(\square\)

**Conversely**, if \(m(t)<0\) persistently, then \(b(z)<0\), so \(z\) is outside the set \(\{b\ge 0\}\). If no independent stabilizer outside \(\mathcal U(\mathbf G)\) is modeled, there is no admissible policy that can keep \(x\) in \(\mathcal S\). Hence the modeled guarantee of invariance is lost.

