## Appendix B: Non-Smooth Analysis and Barrier Functions

### B.1 Clarke's Generalized Gradient

Let \(b:\mathcal Z\to\mathbb R\) be locally Lipschitz. The Clarke generalized gradient of \(b\) at \(z\) is defined as
\[
\partial_C b(z) = \operatorname{conv}\left\{
\lim_{k\to\infty} \nabla b(z_k) :
z_k\to z,\ \nabla b(z_k)\ \text{exists}
\right\}.
\]
It is a nonempty, compact, convex subset of \(\mathbb R^{\dim\mathcal Z}\). The generalized directional derivative is
\[
b^\circ(z;v) = \max_{\zeta\in\partial_C b(z)} \zeta\cdot v.
\]

### B.2 Filippov Solutions

For a differential equation with discontinuous right-hand side,
\[
\dot z = f(z,u,d),
\]
a Filippov solution is an absolutely continuous function \(z(t)\) satisfying
\[
\dot z(t) \in \bigcap_{\delta>0} \bigcap_{\mu(N)=0} \overline{\operatorname{co}}\{f(z(t),u,d): z(t)\in B(z(t),\delta)\setminus N\}
\]
for almost all \(t\). This set-valued map allows the use of Clarke's generalized gradient in the barrier condition.

### B.3 Derivation of the Non-Smooth Barrier Condition

The non-smooth barrier condition is
\[
\max_{v\in\partial_C b(z)}
\left(
v\cdot \dot z
\right)
+
\alpha(b(z))
\ge 0
\qquad
\forall d\in\mathcal D(\mathbf P,\rho,\xi,\tau).
\]
This is equivalent to
\[
b^\circ(z;f(z,u,d)) + \alpha(b(z)) \ge 0
\]
for all \(d\). By Clarke's theorem, if this holds for all \(d\), then the set \(\{z: b(z)\ge 0\}\) is forward invariant under the differential inclusion \(\dot z \in \{f(z,u,d): d\in\mathcal D\}\).

### B.4 Relation to Standard CBF Theory

When \(b\) is continuously differentiable, \(\partial_C b(z)=\{\nabla b(z)\}\), and the condition reduces to the standard CBF condition
\[
\nabla b(z)\cdot f(z,u,d) + \alpha(b(z)) \ge 0.
\]
Thus the non-smooth formulation is a strict generalization.

---

## Appendix C: Hamilton–Jacobi–Isaacs Equation and Level-Set Methods

### C.1 The Reachability Game

Consider the differential game
\[
\dot z = f(z,u,d), \quad u\in\mathcal U(\mathbf G),\ d\in\mathcal D(\mathbf P,\rho,\xi,\tau).
\]
The controller seeks to keep the state in the safe set \(\mathcal S\), while the disturbance seeks to drive it out. The value function \(V(z)\) is defined as
\[
V(z) = \max_{d(\cdot)} \min_{u(\cdot)} \Phi(z(\cdot)),
\]
where \(\Phi\) is the cost functional (e.g., the time to exit \(\mathcal S\)).

### C.2 Viscosity Solutions and the HJI Equation

The value function \(V\) is the unique Lipschitz continuous viscosity solution of the Hamilton–Jacobi–Isaacs equation
\[
\min_{u\in\mathcal U(\mathbf G)}\max_{d\in\mathcal D(\mathbf P,\rho,\xi,\tau)}
\nabla V(z)\cdot f(z,u,d) = 0,
\]
with boundary condition \(V(z)=0\) on \(\partial\mathcal S\). The robust viability kernel is the sub-zero level set:
\[
\mathcal K = \{z: V(z)\le 0\}.
\]

### C.3 Level-Set Approximation

The HJI equation can be solved numerically using level-set methods. The zero sub-level set of \(V\) is evolved backwards in time until convergence. The computational complexity is \(O(N^{\dim\mathcal Z})\), where \(N\) is the number of grid points per dimension. For high-dimensional systems, approximate methods such as deep learning-based PDE solvers or max-plus methods can be used.

### C.4 Computational Complexity

The dimension of the augmented state \(z=(x,\mathbf P,\mathbf G,\rho,\xi,\tau)\) is \(n+5+4+1+1+1 = n+12\). For typical \(n\) (e.g., \(n=2\) to \(10\)), the dimension is between 14 and 22. This is currently beyond the reach of grid-based level-set methods but may be tractable with sparse grids or neural network approximations.

---

## Appendix D: Delay Systems and Functional State Spaces

### D.1 Retarded Functional Differential Equations

A retarded functional differential equation (RFDE) is
\[
\dot x(t) = F(x_t, u(t), d(t)),
\]
where \(x_t\in C([-\tau,0],\mathbb R^n)\) is the history segment \(x_t(\theta)=x(t+\theta)\). The initial condition is a function \(\phi\in C([-\tau,0],\mathbb R^n)\).

### D.2 Viability Kernel in Function Spaces

The safe set in the history space is
\[
\mathcal S_\tau = \{\phi\in C([-\tau,0],\mathbb R^n): \phi(\theta)\in\mathcal S\ \forall\theta\in[-\tau,0]\}.
\]
The robust viability kernel is
\[
\mathcal K_\tau(\mathbf P,\mathbf G,\rho,\xi)
=
\left\{
\phi\in\mathcal S_\tau:
\exists\pi,\ \forall d(\cdot)\in\mathcal D(\mathbf P,\rho,\xi,\tau),\ 
x_t\in\mathcal S_\tau\ \forall t\ge 0
\right\}.
\]

### D.3 Existence Results for Delay Viability

Existence of viable trajectories for delay differential inclusions follows from the viability theorems of Aubin (1991) and Colombo and Rosini (2005). Under Lipschitz conditions on \(F\), the viability kernel \(\mathcal K_\tau\) is nonempty if and only if the tangential condition
\[
F(\phi,u,d)\cap T_{\mathcal S_\tau}(\phi)\neq\emptyset
\]
holds for all \(\phi\in\mathcal S_\tau\) and some \(u\in\mathcal U(\mathbf G)\), for all \(d\in\mathcal D(\mathbf P,\rho,\xi,\tau)\), where \(T_{\mathcal S_\tau}(\phi)\) is the contingent cone to \(\mathcal S_\tau\) at \(\phi\).

### D.4 Approximation of Delay Viability Kernels

Numerical approximation of \(\mathcal K_\tau\) requires discretization of the function space \(C([-\tau,0],\mathbb R^n)\). One approach is to use a finite-dimensional approximation via Chebyshev polynomials or B-splines. The dimension of the approximation grows with \(\tau\), making the problem computationally intensive for large delays.

---

## Appendix E: Governance Manifold and Resource Constraints

### E.1 Definition and Properties of the Governance Manifold

The feasible governance set \(\mathcal M\subset\mathcal G\) is a compact manifold, typically a Pareto frontier defined by a resource constraint
\[
\sum_{i=1}^4 c_i G_i \le R,
\]
where \(c_i>0\) are resource costs and \(R\) is the total available resource. The manifold is
\[
\mathcal M = \left\{\mathbf G\in\mathcal G: \sum_{i=1}^4 c_i G_i = R\right\}.
\]
This is a 3-dimensional simplex embedded in \(\mathbb R^4\).

### E.2 Directional Monotonicity on the Pareto Frontier

On \(\mathcal M\), the partial order \(\succeq\) is not total. Two governance vectors \(\mathbf G_1,\mathbf G_2\in\mathcal M\) are incomparable if neither dominates the other. The directional monotonicity assumption A2 states that for \(\mathbf G_1,\mathbf G_2\in\mathcal M\),
\[
\mathbf G_2\succeq\mathbf G_1 \Longrightarrow \mathcal U(\mathbf G_1)\subseteq\mathcal U(\mathbf G_2).
\]
This holds only along the direction of increasing governance capacity. It may fail if increasing one component requires decreasing another, and the decrease in the other component reduces the admissible control set more than the increase expands it.

### E.3 Trade-offs and the Failure of A2

Consider the resource constraint \(A + K = R\). If \(\mathbf G_1=(A_1,K_1)\) and \(\mathbf G_2=(A_2,K_2)\) with \(A_2>A_1\) and \(K_2<K_1\), then \(\mathbf G_2\) and \(\mathbf G_1\) are incomparable under \(\succeq\). A2 does not apply. If, however, we restrict to the direction of increasing \(A\) while decreasing \(K\), the admissible control set may shrink because correction capacity is reduced. Thus A2 can fail on \(\mathcal M\).

