## Appendix F: Measurement Protocols

### F.1 Anticipation (\(A\))

**Definition.** Anticipation capacity is the fraction of the agent's internal representations that are causally linked to human-intended concepts. It is measured using activation patching and circuit completeness scores.

**Formula.**
\[
A = \frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^N \mathbb{1}\left[\text{activation patch on concept }i\text{ changes output}\right].
\]
Normalized to \([0,1]\).

### F.2 Judgment (\(J\))

**Definition.** Judgment capacity is the worst-case alignment degradation under distribution shift. It is measured using distributionally robust optimization.

**Formula.**
\[
J = \min_{Q\in\mathcal P} \mathbb E_{x\sim Q}[\text{alignment}(x)] - \lambda \operatorname{KL}(Q\|P_0),
\]
where \(\mathcal P\) is a set of distributions around the training distribution \(P_0\), and \(\lambda>0\) is a regularization parameter. Normalized to \([0,1]\).

### F.3 Constraint (\(C\))

**Definition.** Constraint capacity is the fraction of the state space that has been mathematically proven to be outside the agent's actionable set.

**Formula.**
\[
C = \frac{\operatorname{Vol}(\mathcal X_{\text{verified}})}{\operatorname{Vol}(\mathcal X)},
\]
where \(\mathcal X_{\text{verified}}\) is the set of states for which reachability analysis proves that the agent cannot enter. Normalized to \([0,1]\).

### F.4 Correction (\(K\))

**Definition.** Correction capacity is the product of the mean time to recovery and the cascading-failure contagion index.

**Formula.**
\[
K = \left(\frac{\mathrm{MTTR}_{\text{ref}}}{\mathrm{MTTR}}\right) \times \left(1 - \frac{N_{\text{cascaded}}}{N_{\text{total}}}\right),
\]
where \(N_{\text{cascaded}}\) is the number of downstream systems compromised before isolation, and \(N_{\text{total}}\) is the total number of systems. Normalized to \([0,1]\) by a saturating map.

### F.5 Validation and Reliability

All proxies must be validated for inter-rater reliability using Cohen's kappa or intraclass correlation. A minimum kappa of 0.7 is required. Proxies must be normalized to bounded ranges and compared against holistic expert assessments.

