## Appendix G: Proposed Experimental Designs

**Note:** No experiments, physical tests, or code training have been performed. The following are proposed experimental designs for future implementation.

### G.1 Autonomous Laboratory Agents

**Objective.** Test whether the synchronization margin \(m(t)\) predicts exit from the safe operating region.

**Design.** Simulate an autonomous laboratory agent controlling a chemical reaction. Define \(\mathcal S\) as the set of temperature and pressure states within safe limits. Power \(\mathbf P\) corresponds to the agent's scale, autonomy, irreversibility, reach, and amplification. Governance \(\mathbf G\) corresponds to anticipation, judgment, constraint, and correction capacities. Vary \(\mathbf P\) and \(\mathbf G\) systematically. Compute \(m(t)\) using level-set methods. Pre-register a threshold \(m<0\) and measure the time to exit \(\mathcal S\). If exit time correlates with \(m\), the theory is supported.

### G.2 Orbital Robotics

**Objective.** Test the effect of communication latency \(\tau\) on the viability kernel.

**Design.** Simulate a swarm of orbital robots with delayed communication to Earth. Define \(\mathcal S\) as the set of orbital configurations that avoid collisions and maintain coverage. Vary \(\tau\) from 0 to 20 minutes (Mars-Earth delay). Compute \(\mathcal K_\tau\) in the history space. Measure the minimum local governance capacity required for viability. Test whether \(K(\tau)\) decays as \(K_0(1+\tau/\tau_0)^{-\beta}\).

### G.3 Power-Grid Control Systems

**Objective.** Test the monotonicity of the viability kernel with respect to power.

**Design.** Simulate a power grid with increasing renewable penetration (power) and varying governance capacity (anticipation of demand, judgment of grid stability, constraint of load shedding, correction of blackouts). Compute \(\mathcal K(\mathbf P,\mathbf G)\) for different \(\mathbf P\). Test whether \(\mathcal K\) shrinks as \(\mathbf P\) grows and whether \(\mathbf G_{\min}\) increases.

### G.4 Algorithmic Trading Systems

**Objective.** Test the synchronization condition in a high-frequency trading environment.

**Design.** Simulate a trading algorithm with varying speed (power) and risk management (governance). Define \(\mathcal S\) as the set of portfolio values above a threshold. Compute \(m(t)\) and measure exit time. Test whether \(m<0\) predicts financial losses.

### G.5 Historical Case Studies

**Objective.** Test the theory on historical episodes of high power and low governance.

**Design.** Select historical episodes (e.g., introduction of nuclear weapons, industrial revolution, early AI deployment). Code power and governance vectors from historical data. Estimate whether the synchronization condition was violated and whether catastrophe followed. Use process tracing and counterfactual analysis.

### G.6 Simulation Studies for Falsification

**Objective.** Pre-register experiments that intentionally drive \(m(t)<0\) and measure exit time.

**Design.** Use a low-dimensional dynamical model (e.g., \(n=2\)). Generate random power and governance trajectories. Compute \(m(t)\) and record exit times. Test the null hypothesis that exit time is independent of \(m\). Reject the null if the correlation is significant.

